trace.content:
[THOUGHT] The abdominal X-ray shows dilated bowel loops and absence of rectal gas—consistent with distal intestinal obstruction. This supports mechanical or functional obstruction but does not differentiate etiologies. Given the infant’s age, bilious vomiting, and failure to pass meconium since birth, key considerations include:  
- Meconium ileus (CF-associated) → often shows “soap-bubble” or “ground-glass” appearance on X-ray (not described here), but may still be present;  
- Hirschsprung disease → typically presents later (though can be neonatal), and X-ray may show transition zone (not visible on plain film alone);  
- Intestinal atresia or stenosis → usually proximal, but distal atresia is rarer;  
- Malrotation with volvulus → X-ray is insensitive for this; UGI remains gold standard.  

Since UGI was not yet obtained and is essential to rule out volvulus—and no indication of unavailability has been given—I must prioritize it again, as delay risks necrosis. Per protocol, I may re-request if prior attempts yielded “Not specified” (i.e., not “Not available”). This is clinically justified and within turn limits.

[TEST] Upper gastrointestinal series (UGI) with small bowel follow-through

